### Hierarchical Cryptographically Generated Addresses (HCGA)
- **Presentation:** [Hierarchical Cryptographically Generated Addresses](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6man-hierarchical-cryptographically-generated-addresses-02) presented by Nick Buraglio.
- **Draft:** `draft-buraglio-6man-hcga`
- **Technical Content:** Extends RFC 3972 (CGA) to assign cryptographically verifiable IPv6 subnets (e.g., `/64`) to host pods, containers, or hardware components using TPM-bound keys. This provides hardware-to-application network traceability in zero-trust, HPC, or high-security environments.
- **Discussion:**
  - Philip Tiesel raised potential hash collision concerns with 16-bit subnet boundaries under birthday attack bounds and queried support for smaller prefix splits (e.g., `/80`).
  - Joel Jaeggli noted similarities to ECMP host pool allocation strategies used in data center networks.
  - Éric Vyncke asked for clarification on the hash algorithm (SHA-256) and requested an early Security Directorate review.
  - Eric Kline and Bob Hinden agreed that obtaining a Security Directorate review and finding a co-author with cryptography expertise would be appropriate next steps.

