### A RoCEv2 Flow-Level Load Balancing Method Based on the IPv6 Flow Label
- **Presentation:** [A RoCEv2 Flow-Level Load Balancing Method Based on the IPv6 Flow Label](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-6man-a-rocev2-flow-level-load-balancing-method-based-on-the-ipv6-flow-label-00) presented by Weiqiang Cheng.
- **Draft:** `draft-cheng-6man-rocev2-flow-label`
- **Technical Content:** Proposes having data center ingress edge devices populate the IPv6 Flow Label field using the Queue Pair (QP) numbers extracted from RoCEv2 BTH/DTH headers. This allows ECMP routers to distribute parallel RDMA sessions (elephant flows) across DCI links without modifying end-host stacks.
- **Discussion:**
  - Éric Vyncke questioned including IPv6 source/destination addresses in the flow label hash, as ECMP switches already include IP addresses in their 5-tuple hash.
  - Joel Halpern recommended avoiding normative MUST mandates on specific hash algorithms and avoiding strict prohibitions against host-generated flow labels.
  - Jie Dong noted that switches capable of inspecting deep headers can hash on QP numbers directly, and cautioned that reordering RoCEv2 packets across different paths could degrade QP performance.

